      dealing: 'Tiles are shuffled face-down. Each player draws 7 tiles. The remaining 14 tiles form the face-down stock.',
    },
    turnFlow: "1. **Start**: The first tile played determines the trump suit (Blanks). 2. **Tricks**: Players play one tile each. Highest trump wins; if no trump, highest card of led suit wins. 3. **The Draw**: While the stock exists, players draw one tile after each trick. 4. **Trump**: The double-blank (Bingo) is the highest trump, followed by other tiles with blanks. 5. **Closing**: A player may 'close the stock' to stop drawing and force strict suit-following.",
    moveValidityConditions: {
      ante: 'Bingo is a point-based trick-taking game and does not utilize a monetary or chip-based ante.',
      deal: 'Initial tile distribution is performed by the players drawing from the boneyard at the start of the round.',
      reveal_market: 'The stock is a face-down pile of tiles; there is no public market of revealed tiles in this game.',
      buy_market: 'Tiles cannot be purchased from a market; they are only acquired through drawing from the stock after tricks.',
      buy_stock: 'Drawing from the stock is a free mandatory action after each trick until the pile is empty or closed.',
      fold: 'Players must participate in the tricks until someone claims 70 points or the hand is otherwise resolved.',
      check: 'The game uses a trick-taking structure rather than a vying structure where checking would be an option.',
      call: 'There is no pot or bet to match in Bingo; competition is based on trick-taking point accumulation.',
      bet: 'Success is determined by total point accumulation on a scorecard rather than through financial betting.',
      raise: 'There are no wagering rounds in this game, so the value of the deal cannot be increased via raises.',
      declare: 'Only available after winning a trick.',
      reveal_hand: 'Tiles remain private until played into tricks; no separate reveal phase occurs during play.',
      award_pot: 'Winning points are added to a match total rather than distributed from a physical pot of assets.',
      play_card: 'Bingo is a domino game where tiles are played to tricks; card engine logic does not apply.',
      draw: 'Only while stock is open and has tiles remaining.',
      discard: 'Every tile in the player hand must be played into a trick; no tiles are discarded or buried.',
      pass: 'Players must always play a tile to every trick; passing a turn without action is not permitted.',
      bid: 'Bingo does not utilize a bidding system to determine the dealer or game objectives.',
      meld: 'This is a trick-taking game based on tile values rather than creating melds in the hand.',
      go_out: 'Winning occurrs through the 70-point claim rather than a separate go-out engine action.',
    },
    exampleHands: [
      'Setup example: 7 card(s) per player.',
      'Rule example: Win tricks worth at least 70 points to win the deal and accumulate 7 game points to win the match.',
      "Turn-flow example: 1. **Start**: The first tile played determines the trump suit (Blanks). 2. **Tricks**: Players play one tile each. Highest trump wins; if no trump, highest card of led suit wins. 3. **The Draw**: While the stock exists, players draw one tile after each trick. 4. **Trump**: The double-blank (Bingo) is the highest trump, followed by other tiles with blanks. 5. **Closing**: A player may 'close the stock' to stop drawing and force strict suit-following.",
      'Scoring example: Win tricks worth at least 70 points. Game points: 1 (loser has 30+), 2 (loser has <30), 3 (loser won 0 tricks). Match to 7 game points. First to 7 game points across multiple deals..',
